---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import Fig from "../../components/Fig.astro";
import "../../styles/service.css";
import "../../styles/case.css";

const SITE = "https://www.tmdesignengineering.com";
const url = `${SITE}/work/ooni-dough-proofing-boxes`;
const jsonld = [
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "Article",
    "@id": `${url}#article`,
    headline: "Ooni Dough Proofing Trays, from a self-contradicting brief to the production line",
    description: "Case study: design lead and sole design engineer on Ooni's injection-moulded dough proofing trays, from consumer research and the brief to production release, resolving a self-contradicting brief in numbers with a nesting analysis, anti-wedge ribs and statistical tolerance stacks across four stacking combinations. 4.8 stars across 460+ reviews.",
    url,
    author: { "@id": `${SITE}/#thomas-mckee` },
    publisher: { "@id": `${SITE}/#business` },
    about: "Ooni dough proofing trays",
    isPartOf: { "@id": `${SITE}/#website` },
  },
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
    itemListElement: [
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: `${SITE}/` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Work", item: `${SITE}/#work` },
      { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Ooni Dough Proofing Trays", item: url },
    ],
  },
];
---
<Base
  title="Ooni Dough Proofing Trays Case Study, Tolerance & DFM | Thomas McKee"
  description="Case study: sole design engineer on Ooni's injection-moulded dough proofing trays, from the brief to production. A self-contradicting brief resolved in numbers, anti-wedge ribs, and statistical tolerance stacks. 4.8 stars across 460+ reviews."
  type="website"
  jsonld={jsonld}>
<main>
  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="svc-hero">
    <div class="wrap svc-hero-grid">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">Case study · Ooni Dough Proofing Trays</span>
        <h1>A deceptively simple tray, <em>from a contradictory brief to the production line.</em></h1>
        <p class="lede">Ooni's dough proofing trays are a modular, injection-moulded polypropylene system for proofing and storing dough balls. I was design lead and the sole design engineer from consumer research and the brief right through to production release, covering the concept and industrial design, the tolerancing, DFM with the tooling supplier, and production support.</p>
        <div class="svc-hero-cta">
          <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/#contact">Start your project <span class="arw">→</span></a>
          <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="#process">See the process</a>
        </div>
      </div>
      <aside class="spec" aria-label="Project summary">
        <div class="spec-head"><span>Project</span><span>CASE 03</span></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Client</div><div class="spec-v">Ooni</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Role</div><div class="spec-v">Design lead · sole engineer</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Process</div><div class="spec-v">Injection-moulded PP, high volume</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Scope</div><div class="spec-v">Brief to production line</div></div>
        <div class="spec-row"><div class="spec-k">Rating</div><div class="spec-v">4.8★ · 460+ reviews</div></div>
      </aside>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- RESULTS STRIP -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="headline">
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">4.8★</div><div class="hl">across 460+ customer reviews</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">6</div><div class="hl">dough balls in a tray that still fits a domestic fridge</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">4 of 5</div><div class="hl">conflicting requirements met in full, the fifth by a quantified compromise</div></div>
        <div class="hstat"><div class="hn">4</div><div class="hl">stacking combinations, each analysed to a tolerance stack</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROCESS OVERVIEW -->
  <section class="band" id="process">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">The design process</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">How the project ran, end to end</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">A tray sounds like the simplest product in the range, and that's exactly why it's a good example. With no electronics and no moving parts, everything comes down to the brief, the geometry, the tolerances and how it's moulded. As the only engineer on it, I took it through every stage.</p>
      <ol class="proc">
        <li><div><h3>Consumer research and the brief</h3><p>I started with how people actually proof and store dough, and what customers said about the previous tray, then wrote the brief: fits in a fridge, holds six dough balls, nests for storage, and releases dough cleanly.</p><div class="uses">User insights · brief creation</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Turn the brief into a specification</h3><p>Putting numbers on every requirement showed that five of them couldn't all be met at once. I proved that with hand calculations so the trade-off could be decided on evidence.</p><div class="uses">Requirements engineering · hand calculation</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Explore concepts and the industrial design</h3><p>Concepts were sketched and modelled against the conflicting requirements, aiming for a minimalist look that sits well in a modern kitchen.</p><div class="uses">Concept design · industrial design · CMF</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Prototype and test</h3><p>Printed prototypes let me check nesting, stacking, lid fit and dough release by hand before any steel was cut.</p><div class="uses">Rapid prototyping · physical testing</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Tolerance and DFM with the toolmaker</h3><p>Statistical tolerance stacks for every way the parts fit together, plus draft, wall thickness and flow length worked through with the tooling supplier.</p><div class="uses">Tolerance stack analysis · DFM · draft analysis</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Production CAD and the drawing pack</h3><p>Manufacturing-ready CAD and a full technical drawing pack, with all the project documentation managed alongside it.</p><div class="uses">Production CAD · GD&amp;T · technical documentation</div></div></li>
        <li><div><h3>Tooling and production support</h3><p>I supported the supplier through tooling, checked the first articles against the analysis with CMM data, and released the product to production.</p><div class="uses">Supplier collaboration · first-article inspection</div></div></li>
      </ol>
      <div class="cs-toc" aria-label="In this case study">
        <a href="#cs-3-1">3.1 · The specification</a>
        <a href="#cs-3-1-process">Geometry, tolerance &amp; DFM</a>
        <a href="#cs-3-1-results">Results</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- SKILLS -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Skills used</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">What this project called on</h2>
      <div class="skills">
        <div><div class="sk-h">Engineering</div><div class="sk-tags"><span>Requirements engineering</span><span>Hand calculation</span><span>Tolerance stack analysis</span><span>Statistical tolerancing</span><span>DFM</span><span>Concept design</span><span>Industrial design</span><span>CMF</span><span>User insights</span><span>Technical documentation</span></div></div>
        <div><div class="sk-h">Tools</div><div class="sk-tags"><span>SolidWorks</span><span>Draft analysis</span><span>Manufacturing drawings &amp; GD&amp;T</span><span>CMM inspection data</span></div></div>
        <div><div class="sk-h">Manufacturing</div><div class="sk-tags"><span>Injection moulding</span><span>Polypropylene</span><span>Large thin-walled mouldings</span><span>Warp control</span><span>First-article inspection</span><span>High-volume production</span></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 3.1 PROBLEM -->
  <section class="band" id="cs-3-1">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">Case study 3.1 · Nesting, tolerance &amp; DFM</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Making a usable specification from an impossible brief</h2>

      <div class="stage">
        <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Problem</span></div>
        <div class="about-body">
          <p>Each requirement in the brief made sense on its own. The trays had to fit in a fridge for cold proofing, giving a maximum outer envelope of 420 × 320 × 100&nbsp;mm. They had to hold six 250&nbsp;g dough balls, like the old tray, so the inside had to be at least 365 × 265 × 70&nbsp;mm. They had to nest to save space in shipping and storage, they mustn't wedge together, and there could be no internal features, because those make it hard to get the dough out.</p>
          <p>Together, they couldn't all be satisfied. Nesting needs draft, and draft plus fixed inner dimensions eats into the fixed outer envelope. Stopping the trays wedging needs a feature to bottom out on, but "no internal features" rules one out on the inside, and a clean inner face means a clean outer face too. The brief contradicted itself, so my first engineering task was to prove that in numbers rather than argue it in a meeting.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="callout"><b>Requirements as received</b>: fits in a fridge · holds six 250&nbsp;g dough balls · trays nest · no wedging · no internal features.</div>
        <div class="figs">
          <Fig n="59" cap="Required draft against nesting depth, for three wall thicknesses" />
          <Fig n="60" cap="Concepts explored against the conflicting requirements" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 3.1 PROCESS -->
  <section class="band alt" id="cs-3-1-process">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase phase-solution">Process</span></div>
        <h3>Quantify the conflict, then hide the solution in plain sight</h3>
        <ol class="steps-inline">
          <li><span class="sn">01</span><span><b>Write the nesting condition.</b> For one tray to enter another, the inside at the entry plane has to clear the outer wall of the tray above. Over a nesting depth <em>h</em>, at draft angle <em>α</em> and wall thickness <em>t</em>, that's <span class="mono">2h·tan α ≥ 2t + c</span>, with <em>c</em> the running clearance.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">02</span><span><b>Put the trade-off in front of the stakeholders.</b> With the outer and inner dimensions both fixed, <em>h</em> and <em>t</em> were fixed too, leaving draft as the only free variable. I mapped the draft required against nesting height and wall thickness with <strong>hand calculations</strong> and took the chart to the team, so the decision was made on the numbers.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">03</span><span><b>Make the anti-wedge feature something users want anyway.</b> Draft alone means trays jam on the taper. I added external ribs on the tray wall, designed as handles, so the tray bottoms out on the ribs instead. They stop the wedge, stiffen a large thin-walled moulding against warp, and leave a gap to get your fingers under.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">04</span><span><b>Tolerance every way the parts meet.</b> Lid on tray (seal and rattle), lid in tray (drop-in clearance), tray in tray (nest depth and wedge) and tray on lid (stack stability) are four different loops through the same features, so each got its own <strong>tolerance stack</strong>. Contributors included cavity dimensions, mould shrinkage across a large PP part, warp from uneven cooling, base flatness and the position of the rib seats.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">05</span><span><b>Turn pass/fail into a scrappage rate.</b> Treating each contributor as normal and independent, <span class="mono">σ<sub>gap</sub> = √Σσ<sub>i</sub>²</span> and <span class="mono">Z = (g<sub>min</sub> − μ<sub>gap</sub>) / σ<sub>gap</sub></span>, so <span class="mono">Φ(Z)</span> predicts the fraction of assemblies that wedge or rattle. That let me tighten the rib seat tolerance where it was worth paying for and open everything else up.</span></li>
          <li><span class="sn">06</span><span><b>Work through the DFM with the toolmaker.</b> <strong>Draft analysis</strong> on the production part, and a wall thickness, flow and stiffness trade: a 250:1 flow-length ratio for the chosen PP grade over a 310&nbsp;mm longest flow path meant a wall of at least 1.25&nbsp;mm to fill in theory. Tapered internal walls were engineered for clean, damage-free dough release.</span></li>
        </ol>
        <div class="figs f3">
          <Fig n="62" cap="Nesting and draft hand calculations, notebook" />
          <Fig n="63" cap="Ribs as handles, bottoming out in the nest" />
          <Fig n="65" cap="The four stacking combinations" />
          <Fig n="64" cap="Gap distribution against the wedge and rattle limits" />
          <Fig n="66" cap="Draft analysis on the production part" />
          <Fig n="67" cap="Wall thickness, flow and stiffness trade in the DFM review" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CS 3.1 RESULTS -->
  <section class="band" id="cs-3-1-results">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="stage">
        <div class="phase-row"><span class="phase">Results</span></div>
        <h3>Requirements met, a compromise found for the conflict, and into production</h3>
        <div class="mini"><div><b>4.8★</b>460+ reviews</div><div><b>6</b>dough balls per tray</div><div><b>Low</b>scrappage, as predicted</div></div>
        <div class="about-body">
          <p>The trays nest without wedging, bottom out on a defined feature, have clean internal faces and fit in a domestic fridge. They hold six dough balls and are tall enough to bulk prove them too, and they work with the Pizza Prep Lid to extend the range.</p>
          <p>The scrappage the stack predicted was borne out in production, with low scrappage and low failure, and CMM inspection of the first articles confirmed the rib seats and base flatness sat where the analysis said they would. The trays now sit at 4.8 stars across 460+ reviews.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="figs f3">
          <Fig n="68" cap="Trays stack and nest, lids stack and nest" />
          <Fig n="69" cap="Stacked trays" />
          <Fig n="70" cap="Hero shot" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- HOW I HELP -->
  <section class="band alt">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="sec-idx">How I could help you</span>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The same process, on your product</h2>
      <p class="sec-intro">Turning a wish list into a specification you can actually build, then proving the tolerances before the tool is cut, is the core of <a href="/services/manufacturing-preparation" style="color:var(--ember)">Manufacturing Preparation</a>. If your brief has requirements pulling against each other, a <a href="/services/feasibility-study" style="color:var(--ember)">Feasibility Study</a> is a good place to find out early.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" id="start">
    <div class="wrap">
      <div class="cta">
        <div class="inner">
          <span class="eyebrow">Let's talk</span>
          <h2 style="margin-top:14px">Have a moulded part to get right first time?</h2>
          <p>Book a free intro call and tell me about it. I'll be honest about where the risks are and how I can help.</p>
          <div class="row">
            <a class="btn btn-primary btn-lg" href="/#contact">Book a free intro call <span class="arw">→</span></a>
            <a class="btn btn-ghost btn-lg" href="/#work">See more work</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
</Base>
